{"key": "lineup", "text": "These are some of the rockets that put them there, side by side, at one scale. From the little Juno I that launched Explorer 1, past the Saturn V that took Apollo to the Moon, to Starship, the biggest rocket ever flown. And this is what they carried, in the order they flew. First Sputnik, then Explorer and Vanguard, and the first probes to the Moon. Then the first people in orbit, the first probe to another planet, the first walk in space, and the first satellites to carry television across oceans. Apollo 11, and footprints on the Moon. Through the seventies, the first space stations, landers on Mars, and Voyager 1, now farther away than anything else we have built. Then the Space Shuttle, Mir, and Hubble, and the International Space Station, where crews have been on board without a break since 2000. This century brought rovers on Mars, China's first astronaut, a landing on a comet, and India at Mars on its first try. Then the strangest payload of all: on the first flight of Falcon Heavy, a cherry red Tesla Roadster, with a dummy astronaut called Starman at the wheel. It's still out there, circling the Sun. And now, thousands of Starlink satellites, the James Webb telescope, people flying around the Moon again, and Starship. Sixty-nine years. Nearly seven thousand launches. This is everything we've ever launched.", "take": ["take.mp3", 223.235, 329.192], "alignment": {"characters": ["T", "h", "e", "s", "e", " ", "a", "r", "e", " ", "s", "o", "m", "e", " ", "o", "f", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "r", "o", "c", "k", "e", "t", "s", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "p", "u", "t", " ", "t", "h", "e", "m", " ", "t", "h", "e", "r", "e", ",", " ", "s", "i", "d", "e", " ", "b", "y", " ", "s", "i", "d", "e", ",", " ", "a", "t", " ", "o", "n", "e", " ", "s", "c", "a", "l", "e", ".", " ", "F", "r", "o", "m", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "l", "i", "t", "t", "l", "e", " ", "J", "u", "n", "o", " ", "O", "n", "e", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "l", "a", "u", "n", "c